AB  - Multimodal knowledge graph completion (MMKGC) has recently become an important research task, aiming to fill missing knowledge and thereby enhance the completeness of knowledge representation and the accuracy of reasoning. However, existing methods often rely heavily on dataset-specific knowledge, which limits their reasoning performance. To address this issue, this paper proposes a novel framework, Rationale-Guided Reasoning with LLM for Multimodal Knowledge Graph Completion (RMKGC). The framework consists of two core modules: MMKG and LLM-based Rationale Encoding and Rationale-guided Interaction and Reasoning. The former generates and encodes rationales with large language models (LLMs) without additional training, providing high-quality reasoning cues to complement multimodal entity and relation features. The latter leverages rationales to guide feature interaction and decoupled reasoning, thereby overcoming the limitation of existing methods in preserving modality-specific features. Experiments on three benchmark datasets of different scales demonstrate that the proposed method achieves state-of-the-art performance.
