AB  - Inductive knowledge graph completion (KGC) aims to predict missing triples containing unseen entities. Current mainstream methods mainly rely on reasoning paths and neighborhood structures between entities to construct contextual semantic representations, which struggles to effectively handle real-world scenarios with sparse structures or cold starts. Furthermore, most methods ignore explicit ontology semantics in the KG, which is crucial for ensuring the consistency and robustness of reasoning. To address these issues, we propose a novel and unified Ontology-enhanced Subgraph Reasoning with Prompt Learning model namely OneSRPL. The OneSRPL framework consists of four key components: (i) a Hypergraph-based Ontology Training Module (HOTM), which learns high-order ontological representations to alleviate representation bias under limited structural evidence; (ii) a Subgraph Modeling Module (SMM) that explicitly injects ontology-derived features into entity initialization during subgraph encoding, thereby compensating for sparse or incomplete reasoning paths; (iii) a Contrastive Learning-based Concept Constraint module (CLCC), which enforces semantic consistency between subgraph embeddings and their corresponding concepts at both the entity and relation levels; and (iv) an Ontology-enhanced Prompt Learning Module (OPLM), which transforms structural paths and ontological rules into textual prompts and leverages pre-trained language models to provide stable semantic guidance when structural evidence is insufficient. Experimental results on multiple benchmarks demonstrate the superiority of the proposed model over state-of-the-art baselines, particularly under few-shot settings.
