AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) and Generative AI are a paradigmatic change in medicine that can offer superior diagnostic support, clinical decision-making, and patient-centered care. The chapter is a detailed description of multimodal intelligence systems, which combine textual, visual, and temporal medical data with the aim of optimizing clinical processes. We look closely at the technical underpinnings of healthcare-specific LLMs, with a focus on such popular models as Med-PaLM and BioGPT, and discuss any concerning issues regarding implementation in a clinical context. Applications expand into areas such as medical imaging interpretation, clinical documentation, drug discovery, and treatment planning. Our key considerations comprise data privacy within the context of regulations such as HIPAA and GDPR, FDA AI/ML-based Software as a Medical Device, and AI/ML-based decision-making bias detection and remediation, as well as ethical considerations of the AI-based decision in the healthcare context. The chapter finishes with emerging trends of federated learning, real-time clinical decision support systems, and AI–human collaborative care models that overcome current limitations due to trust, data sharing, and explain ability of the decision. This publication can be used by healthcare providers, researchers, and developers of technology to guide them to learn more about and adopt LLM-based solutions in medical practice.
