AB  - Although Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at semantic understanding, reasoning, and spatial perception in unknown environments, they often struggle to perceive personalized physical layouts and interact with the environment in real time. To address this limitation, this paper proposes a scene-based Multimodal Knowledge Graph (MKG) that integrates LLMs with visual perception modules to predict objects and generate practical action plans. We further introduce a hierarchical Vision-Language-Action (VLA) navigation strategy that combines environmental understanding with the commonsense reasoning of long-context Vision-Language Models (VLMs), along with a robust low-level policy based on topological graphs, enhancing both navigation performance and task planning completeness. At the high level, a long-context VLM processes demonstrated scene episodes and multimodal user instructions to identify the target frame. At the low level, the system uses this target frame and a pre-built topological graph to generate concrete robot control commands at each timestep. Experimental results demonstrate that the action planning generated by the MKG framework outperforms state-of-the-art methods across multiple tasks, highlighting its effectiveness and potential for real-world deployment in complex indoor environments.
