AB  - The interaction of blast events with civil structures within the built environment is a multiphysics and multidisciplinary demanding challenge that spans beyond the traditional boundaries of structural engineering. For this reason, this overview introduces a novel framework for breaking down the blast phenomena, by adopting a new Source–Path–Receiver (SPR) categorization. The ’Source’ is related to the explosion itself, the ’Path’ refers to the media through which the blast wave propagates, the ’Receiver’ is the structure subjected to the blast load. The SPR framework enables a structured literature review, facilitating insights from disciplines adjacent to civil engineering and offering a holistic view of the state of the art regarding underlying physics, modeling approaches and design principles of Blast Mitigation Measures. The vast literature including over 7400 articles is systematically mapped thanks to a novel approach that leverages Large Language Models (LLMs) for content mining. Finally, promising paths for research and innovation in the field of blast effects on built structures are proposed.
