KW  - social network
KW  - graph neural network
KW  - celebrity nodes
KW  - regional features
KW  - geolocation inference
AB  - Social user geolocation is challenged by implicit geographic semantic ambiguity and inherent structural distortion caused by conventional Euclidean graph embedding. Existing social graph learning methods cannot effectively distinguish different types of celebrity users, fail to fully explore geographic correlation between users and regional topology, and suffer from relatively high computational overhead. To address these issues, this paper proposes a Regional Association and Relationship Filtering (RARF) framework. This method develops a geographic distribution statistics combined with LLM semantic parsing strategy, which adaptively identifies global, local, and touring celebrity categories, removes geographically scattered global celebrities from the social graph, and softly down-weights the edges of touring celebrities, thereby suppressing cross-regional geographic noise while preserving valuable location-sensitive local connections. A region-aware feature learning mechanism is further designed to capture relative positional association between users and geographic areas. Moreover, a Hyperbolic Simplified Graph Convolution (HSGC) is constructed, which migrates simplified graph convolution to Lorentzian manifold to fit hierarchical social network topology while maintaining linear computational complexity. Extensive experiments are conducted on two real-world datasets. Compared with the strongest baselines, RARF improves Acc@161 by 0.3 and 0.1 percentage points and reduces the average distance error by 3 km (0.6%) and 4 km (1.0%) on GeoText and Twitter-US, respectively. Although the absolute gains are modest, all improvements are statistically significant (p < 0.05, paired t-test over five independent runs). Sensitivity and ablation experiments verify that the optimal celebrity neighbor threshold, geographic distance threshold, geographic dispersion threshold, and graph propagation layer are all adaptively determined according to data distribution, rather than manual empirical setting. Efficiency results show that RARF reduces training time by 11.8% compared with standard GCN, achieving an excellent trade-off between localization accuracy and computational cost.
