AB  - Traditional bridge defect detection relies on manual visual inspection, which is inefficient and hard to scale. Recent computer vision and deep learning methods can automatically recognize defects but are limited to classification and localization of individual defects, lacking a systematic quantitative assessment of overall bridge health. To address this, this study proposes an intelligent diagnosis system for bridge defect, whose core is a mechanism-constrained large language model-based framework that integrates code provisions and defect mechanisms. The proposed system consists of four stages: input, transformation, analysis, and output. It can automatically identify defect information from images and convert it into structured natural language descriptions. Within the analysis stage, a large language model (LLM) based reasoning engine performs in‑depth analysis of the natural language information through a four‑layer framework: the Specification Layer, the Mechanism Layer, the Optimization Layer, and the Judgment Layer. While strictly adhering to code provisions, this framework also fully incorporates the mechanical and evolutionary mechanisms of defects, thereby significantly improving the accuracy and reliability of diagnostic results. Furthermore, the complete workflow of the system, from input to output, was demonstrated through practical bridge case studies. For validation, three rounds of independent tests were conducted on 45 in-service bridges, and the diagnostic outputs of the system were systematically compared with manual diagnoses provided by multiple authoritative experts. The results show that, under mechanism constraints, the system achieves high precision and good consistency, confirming its effectiveness for bridge defect diagnosis in practical engineering.
