AB  - Multimodal Temporal Reasoning (MTR) is a critical capability for artificial intelligence systems operating in dynamic, real-world environments. While existing studies have treated multimodal learning and temporal reasoning in isolation, this review offers the first comprehensive synthesis of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) for temporal reasoning tasks. We introduce a novel two-level taxonomy that systematically organizes MTR architectures along two complementary axes: fusion strategy and temporal encoding mechanisms. The study progresses from fundamental concepts to sophisticated applications, examining modality-specific temporal encoding, multimodal temporal interaction, and the critical challenges of temporal alignment. Through detailed analysis of healthcare applications and cross-domain comparisons, we demonstrate how three core paradigms enable complex temporal reasoning in the clinical, financial, and environmental domains: (1) Large Language Models (LLMs) as unified decoders that directly consume aligned sequences for end-to-end prediction; (2) LLMs as orchestration agents that plan and execute reasoning by leveraging external tools; and (3) LLMs as semantic guides that condition external generative models to produce interpretable predictions. The study also explores specialized change detection capabilities across morphological, rhythmic, and semantic dimensions, while critically evaluating the limitations of current assessment metrics and explainability methods. Finally, this survey outlines a forward-looking research roadmap that integrates ethical imperatives with practical deployment challenges and emerging trends in temporal MLLMs, thus establishing a fundamental framework for future research in this rapidly evolving field. This framework provides an essential basis for developing more reliable and interpretable temporal MLLMs, which is a crucial step toward their safe deployment in real-world and critical applications such as medical diagnosis, autonomous systems, and financial forecasting.
