AB  - The core of text–video retrieval is to establish precise semantic associations between text and video so that the machine can understand the correspondence between the text description and the visual content of the video. Existing models are prone to mismatches between correct syntax and deviated semantics, and to ignoring fine-grained information in the video, such as specific actions and character interactions. They usually focus on a single temporal scale and have difficulty accounting for local details and the overall background, thereby affecting the accuracy of text–video retrieval. To address the limitations of the above methods, we propose HNMA, a text–video retrieval framework based on cross-modal hard negatives and multi-grained attention, which is designed to enhance fine-grained feature representation and multi-grained semantic understanding. First, HNMA employs Part-of-Speech analysis and language models to perform multiple semantic perturbations on textual descriptions, constructing linguistically fluent yet factually incorrect hard negatives. Although these negatives are generated on the text side, they target video-event structures such as action–object binding, and serve as fine-grained supervisory signals in video-to-text contrastive learning, thereby strengthening cross-modal alignment and the model’s capacity to represent fine-grained visual content. Second, we propose a multi-grained attention mechanism that fuses short, medium, and long windows to model videos at different time scales, thus enhancing the model’s multi-grained semantic representation. Third, we filter the TOP-K key video frame features through the intentional selective attention mechanism to reduce information interference. Extensive experiments on MSRVTT, DiDeMo, and MSVD show that, when built on the T-MASS baseline under a shared ViT-B/32 setting, HNMA delivers clear gains in recall and ranking quality without external scene taxonomies.
