AB  - Various tools (e.g., algorithms, models, and approaches) for interdependent infrastructure networks (IIN) recovery have been developed for human users, such as assessing facility importance, determining recovery sequences, allocating resources, and more. However, these tools lack task-resolving capabilities, depending on human selection and execution to accomplish specific tasks. Hence, this study proposes a TTT (Toolkit, TS-ReAct agents, Task set) framework to develop agents that intelligently use IIN recovery tools like humans. The framework involves (i) a Toolkit containing IIN recovery tools with a corresponding tool knowledge graph, (ii) TS-ReAct (Tool Selection-Reasoning and Acting) agents driven by 8 large language models (LLMs) to manipulate IIN recovery tools, and (iii) a Task set for evaluating and comparing agents’ performance. The findings demonstrate that the best-performing LLM-driven agents achieve a task success rate of up to 1.0. The agents following TS-ReAct pattern outperform those with traditional ReAct pattern by increasing task success rates by 82.2% and reducing excess steps by 88.7%. Besides proposing the TTT framework, this paper also contributes to addressing the conventional agents’ choice paralysis within a tool pool, as well as providing a reusable toolkit and a task set that enrich research on IIN recovery agents. Practically, this work broadens the user scope of IIN recovery tools beyond humans to include agents, potentially enabling IIN managers to interact with agents for indirect tool utilization.
