AB  - Federated Learning (FL) constitutes a decentralized collaborative training framework which permits heterogeneous clients to perform local model training without directly exposing local data, enabling both privacy preservation and collaborative optimization. Large-scale pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs) exhibit remarkable representational power and cross-modal generalization, offering substantial advantages across diverse downstream tasks. Nevertheless, in image-based tasks, pronounced disparities in visual distributions across clients and insufficient pixel-level representations exacerbate the inherent heterogeneity of FL. Moreover, limitations in computational capacity and communication bandwidth further constrain the integration of large-scale models within federated settings. To mitigate the aforementioned challenges, here we propose a novel framework, Hierarchical Adaptive Prompt Learning (FedHAP), which adaptively adjusts the model’s parameters using prompts to accommodate VLMs in federated cross-domain scenarios and utilizes locally learned prompts as a secure conduit for client-side knowledge sharing. FedHAP embeds prompts into both deep and shallow layers of the vision encoder and projects into the text encoder. Furthermore, the framework integrates two optimization stages: local adaptive prompt decoupling and global adaptive prompt aggregation. Initially, local adaptive prompt decoupling is performed at the client to compress high-dimensional text and visual features to low-dimensional representations, effectively mitigating individual heterogeneity. Subsequently, the server conducts attention-based global aggregation to enhance cross-domain generalization. Extensive experiments on diverse heterogeneous benchmark datasets demonstrate that FedHAP outperforms leading adaptive federated learning methods, achieving better personalization, efficient communication, and strong cross-domain generalization.
ER  - 

