AB  - Accurately identifying critical energy retrofit targets serves as the prerequisite for formulating effective commercial building conservation schemes, yet this diagnostic process necessitates the integration of multi-dimensional technical analyses across building envelope performance and building energy system performance with regulatory compliance requirements and expert experiential judgment, historically depending on labor-intensive engineering audits. Large language models (LLMs) offer transformative potential to automate such knowledge-intensive decision workflows through advanced natural language reasoning; however, their operational reliability in retrofit applications remains constrained by limitations in embedding formalized domain-specific knowledge and generating transparent, rule-compliant reasoning pathways. To address these challenges, this study established a knowledge-driven retrofit decision support framework by first formalizing diagnostic criteria from national standards, industry codes, and expert knowledge into a structured rule base covering three core dimensions: building envelope heat transfer coefficients, equipment selection parameters, and operating strategy designs. These engineering rules were then converted into machine-executable training corpora with compliance verification labels. Through Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) fine-tuning of multiple LLMs combined with chain-of-thought prompting and self-consistency mechanisms, the framework enabled rapid identification of critical retrofit targets in existing buildings and generation of interpretable retrofit action plans with explicit decision rationales. Performance evaluation on the test set demonstrated that the FT-GPT-4o model, fine-tuned at a cost of $2.65, achieved 100% rule application accuracy and 84.21% classification accuracy. Subsequent testing with comprehensive data from three real commercial buildings confirmed that the system produced decisions consistent with expert judgments while reducing decision-making time from minutes to seconds compared to manual evaluations. These findings demonstrate that the proposed framework transforms labor-intensive commercial building energy retrofit audits into AI-augmented workflows, automating deficiency identification across multi-domain systems to provide fully traceable, expert-level recommendations.
