AB  - The rapid emergence of new transport modes, driven by innovations such as on-demand ride services, autonomous vehicles, and flying cars, is reshaping our urban mobility landscape. In addition, Mobility-as-a-Service providers frequently update bundled offerings, further expanding the range of travel mode choices. In such dynamic environments, traditional transport mode choice prediction (TMCP) models often fall short, primarily due to limited data on newly introduced modes and their reliance on fixed and static choice sets. These challenges highlight the need for adaptive prediction models capable of incorporating new options as they arise. To address this issue, we formulate the incremental TMCP (ITMCP) problem and propose a novel algorithm framework, ITMCP with LLM-Augmented replay (ITMCP-LAR), that continually adapts to emerging transport modes while preserving knowledge of established ones. ITMCP-LAR integrates real-time streaming data processing, LLM-driven few-shot data augmentation for new modes, a dynamically expandable neural classifier, and a replay-based incremental learning module to prevent catastrophic forgetting. Empirical results on real-world mobility data demonstrate that ITMCP-LAR consistently outperforms traditional static models, delivering superior predictive accuracy and effectively balancing stability and adaptability in rapidly evolving transport environments.
